@style(../styles/IDocDemo.style)
@title(Code 4: @@lab and its submit modes)

`@@lab(TYPE, ...)` is one name for every studio; `@@lab(code, ...)` is a code exercise and `@@lab(octo, ...)` the Octo studio.

@section(A practice lab)

With no `submit`, a lab is practice: the student runs it as often as they like and nothing is recorded.

@lab(code, python)
print("practice only")
@end

@section(Handed in to the server)

`submit=server` adds a Submit button; with `id=` and `pts=` the server re-runs the checks on the submitted source and records the mark.

@lab(code, python, submit=server, id=lab_sum, pts=4, expect="55")
print(sum(range(11)))
@end

@section(Starting from a file)

`file=` loads the starting code from a file; the language comes from its suffix.

@lab(code, file=04-lab.assets/first.sv, submit=server, id=lab_sv, pts=4)
@end

@section(Fixed input)

`stdin=` feeds the same input to every run, so the expected output is fixed.

@lab(code, python, stdin="3 4", submit=server, id=lab_in, pts=2, expect="7")
a, b = map(int, input().split())
print(a + b)
@end

@section(Hidden tests and a solution)

A lab takes @@tests and @@solution like any runnable block.

@lab(code, python, submit=server, id=lab_tests, pts=3)
def square(x):
    return x * x
@tests
assert square(3) == 9
assert square(-2) == 4
@end

@section(The Octo lab)

`@@lab(octo, run)` is the Octo studio; `benchmark=` times it.

@lab(octo, run, benchmark=octo_lab)
A = magic(4);
disp(sum(A(:)))
@end
